nant: cos(x)(-sin(x + \u03c0/8)) - cos(x + \u03c0/8)(-sin(x))\n\n= -cos(x) sin(x + \u03c0/8) + cos(x + \u03c0/8) sin(x)\n\n= sin(x) cos(x + \u03c0/8) - cos(x) sin(x + \u03c0/8) = sin(x - (x + \u03c0/8)) = sin(-\u03c0/8) = -sin(\u03c0/8)\n\nTherefore, the minor determinant is -sin(\u03c0/8). Therefore, the third term is sin(x - \u03c0/8) * (-sin(\u03c0/8)).\n\nPutting it all together:\n\ndet(M) = sin(x) * (\u221a2/2) - sin(x + \u03c0/8) * sin(\u03c0/8) + sin(x - \u03c0/8) * (-sin(\u03c0/8))\n\n= (\u221a2/2) sin(x) - sin(x + \u03c0/8) sin(\u03c0/8) - sin(x - \u03c0/8) sin(\u03c0/8)\n\nNow, let's compute the terms with sin(\u03c0/8). Let's factor out sin(\u03c0/8):\n\n= (\u221a2/2) sin(x) - sin(\u03c0/8) [sin(x + \u03c0/8) + sin(x - \u03c0/8)]\n\nBut earlier, we saw that sin(x + \u03c0/8) + sin(x - \u03c0/8) = 2 sin(x) cos(\u03c0/8). Therefore, substituting that in:\n\n= (\u221a2/2) sin(x) - sin(\u03c0/8) [2 sin(x) cos(\u03c0/8)]\n\n= (\u221a2/2) sin(x) - 2 sin(\u03c0/8) cos(\u03c0/8) sin(x)\n\nWe know that 2 sin A cos A = sin(2A), so 2 sin(\u03c0/8) cos(\u03c0/8) = sin(\u03c0/4) = \u221a2/2. Therefore:\n\n= (\u221a2/2) sin(x) - (\u221a2/2) sin(x) = 0\n\nTherefore, the determinant is indeed zero. So both methods confirm that the Wronskian is zero.\n\nTherefore, the conclusion is that the Wronskian determinant of these three functions is zero. This implies that the functions are linearly dependent on the interval they are defined, which in this case is the entire real line. As we saw earlier, sin(x) can be expressed as a linear combination of sin(x + \u03c0/8) and sin(x - \u03c0/8), so they are not linearly independent. Hence, their Wronskian being zero makes sense.\n\nI think that covers all the steps. I first thought of the structure of the Wronskian matrix, noticed the third row is a multiple of the first, leading to determinant zero. Then, as a check, I expanded the determinant explicitly using cofactor expansion, and after some trigonometric manipulations, confirme