ght by Liang Liang and Hong Hong follows a ratio of 3:4. This means that:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Fish caught by Liang Liang from the first pond} = \\frac{3}{3+4}x = \\frac{3}{7}x\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Fish caught by Hong Hong from the first pond} = \\frac{4}{3+4}x = \\frac{4}{7}x\n   \\]\n\n3. For the second pond, the problem states that Liang Liang catches 33 more fish than in the first pond, in a ratio of 5:3 to what Hong Hong catches. Therefore:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Fish caught by Liang Liang from the second pond} = \\frac{5}{5+3}x = \\frac{5}{8}x\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Fish caught by Hong Hong from the second pond} = \\frac{3}{5+3}x = \\frac{3}{8}x\n   \\]\n\n4. Given that Liang Liang catches 33 more fish in the second pond than in the first pond, we can write:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{5}{8}x - \\frac{3}{7}x = 33\n   \\]\n\n5. To solve for \\( x \\), find a common denominator for the fractions:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{5}{8}x - \\frac{3}{7}x = 33 \n   \\]\n   The least common multiple of 8 and 7 is 56, so:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{5 \\times 7}{56}x - \\frac{3 \\times 8}{56}x = 33\n   \\]\n   Simplifying this:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{35}{56}x - \\frac{24}{56}x = 33\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{11}{56}x = 33\n   \\]\n\n6. Solving for \\( x \\), multiply both sides by 56/11:\n   \\[\n   x = 33 \\cdot \\frac{56}{11}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x = 3 \\cdot 56 \n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x = 168\n   \\]\n\n7. Therefore, each pond has 168 goldfish.\n\nConclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{B}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Calculate the arc length of the curve given by the equation in polar coordinates.\n\n$$\n\\rho = 2 \\cos \\varphi, \\quad 0 \\leq \\varphi \\leq \\frac{\\pi}{6}\n$$", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find the arc length of a polar curve given by \u03c1 = 2 cos \u03c6, where \u03c6 ranges from 0 to \u03c0/6. Hmm, let me recall how to calculate arc length in polar coordinates. I think the formula involves integrating 