\n   \\text{Total words} = 2^7 = 128\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the total number of \"pronounceable\" words:**\n   We shall count the number of words that do **not** contain \\( AAA \\) or \\( BBB \\).\n\n   Consider a recursive approach by looking at the sub-problems of smaller lengths:\n   - Let \\( p(n) \\) represent the number of pronounceable words of length \\( n \\).\n\n   When forming a pronounceable word of length \\( n \\):\n   - The sequence can end in two different ways:\n     - With \\( A \\) or \\( B \\)\n     - The word ending in \\( AA \\) or \\( BB \\)\n\n   This creates possible constructions:\n   - If the word ends in \\( A \\), the previous must end in \\( A \\) (since the sequence \\( AAA \\) is not allowed).\n   - If the word ends in \\( B \\), the previous must end in \\( B \\) (since the sequence \\( BBB \\) is not allowed).\n   \n   Therefore, we can write a recurrence for \\( p(n) \\) based on excluding unpronounceable sequences:\n   \\[\n   p(n) = p(n-1) + p(n-2)\n   \\]\n   \n3. **Base cases:**\n   \\[\n   p(1) = 2 \\quad \\text{(A and B)}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   p(2) = 4 \\quad \\text{(AA, AB, BA, BB)}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the next terms:**\n   - For \\( p(3) \\):\n     \\[\n     p(3) = p(2) + p(1) = 4 + 2 = 6\n     \\]\n   - For \\( p(4) \\):\n     \\[\n     p(4) = p(3) + p(2) = 6 + 4 = 10\n     \\]\n   - For \\( p(5) \\):\n     \\[\n     p(5) = p(4) + p(3) = 10 + 6 = 16\n     \\]\n   - For \\( p(6) \\):\n     \\[\n     p(6) = p(5) + p(4) = 16 + 10 = 26\n     \\]\n   - For \\( p(7) \\):\n     \\[\n     p(7) = p(6) + p(5) = 26 + 16 = 42\n     \\]\n\n5. **Find the number of unpronounceable words:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{Unpronounceable words} = \\text{Total words} - p(7) = 128 - 42 = 86\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\nThe total number of unpronounceable words of length 7 is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{86}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Given \\( f(x) = |1 - 2x|, x \\in [0, 1] \\), the