ion:\n\nN = (999,999f)/(10f -1)\n\nWe need N to be a six-digit integer. So for each f from 1 to 9, compute (999,999f)/(10f -1) and check if it is an integer and six digits.\n\nFor f=1:\n\n(999,999*1)/9 = 111,111. Which is six digits. Okay.\n\nf=2:\n\n(999,999*2)/19 = 1,999,998/19 \u2248105,263.05. Not integer. So no.\n\nf=3:\n\n(999,999*3)/29 \u22482,999,997/29\u2248103,448.17. Not integer.\n\nf=4:\n\n(999,999*4)/39=3,999,996/39=102,564. Exactly. Which is six digits.\n\nf=5:\n\n(999,999*5)/49\u22484,999,995/49\u2248102,040.71. Not integer.\n\nf=6:\n\n(999,999*6)/59\u22485,999,994/59\u2248101,694.81. Not integer.\n\nf=7:\n\n(999,999*7)/69\u22486,999,993/69\u2248101,449.17. Not integer.\n\nf=8:\n\n(999,999*8)/79\u22487,999,992/79\u2248101,265.72. Not integer.\n\nf=9:\n\n(999,999*9)/89\u22488,999,991/89\u2248101,123.49. Not integer.\n\nSo only f=1 and f=4 give integer values for N, which are six-digit numbers. Therefore, the solutions are 111111 and 102564.\n\nBut wait, let's confirm these numbers satisfy the original condition.\n\nFor 111111:\n\nMoving the last digit 1 to front gives 111111. Then f=1, so 1*111111=111111. Correct.\n\nFor 102564:\n\nMoving the last digit 4 to front gives 410256. Then f=4, 4*102564=410256. Correct.\n\nAre there any other numbers? It seems only these two. Let me verify again for f=7.\n\nWait, suppose f=7, and 10f -1=69. Let's compute N=(999,999*7)/69= (999,999/69)*7.\n\nCompute 999,999 \u00f769. 69*14,492=999,948. Remainder 51. So 999,999=69*14,492 +51. So 999,999/69=14,492 +51/69\u224814,492.739. Then multiply by 7: 14,492.739*7\u2248101,449.17. Not an integer. So N is not integer. So no.\n\nSimilarly for other f's.\n\nTherefore, the only solutions are 111111 and 102564.\n\nBut wait, let me check if there's a mistake here. Because the problem states six-digit numbers, and both 111111 and 102564 are six-digit numbers. But maybe there are more? Let's think differently.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there's a cyclic number property here. The nu