y, if we take A such that many elements are the same. But since A is a set, they must be distinct. So we can't have repeated elements. Therefore, we need another approach.\n\nWait, maybe if we use the concept of additive energy. A set with high additive energy has many pairs (x, y) and (u, v) such that x + y = u + v. To minimize the number of elements in S, we need high additive energy. But how do we construct such a set?\n\nOne example is taking A as a union of arithmetic progressions with the same difference. For example, if A is the union of multiple arithmetic progressions that share the same common difference. Let me see. Suppose we have A = {0, 2, 4, ..., 98} union {1, 3, 5, ..., 99}. Then, the sumset would include all even numbers from 0 to 198, which is 100 elements. Wait, but each sum would be even, and the number of distinct sums would be 100 (from 0 to 198 in steps of 2). Wait, but 0 to 198 inclusive with step 2 is (198 - 0)/2 + 1 = 100 elements. But A has 100 elements here, but in this case, S would have 100 elements. But 100 is less than 199. Wait, but that contradicts our previous thought that the minimal is 199. What's going on here?\n\nWait, hold on. If A is all even numbers or all odd numbers, then the sumset would consist of even numbers. For example, if A is all even numbers from 0 to 198, then S would be all even numbers from 0 to 396, but in our case, A is 100 elements. Wait, no, in the example above, A is 50 even numbers and 50 odd numbers. Wait, no, in my previous example, A is {0,2,...,98} (50 elements) and {1,3,...,99} (50 elements), total 100 elements. Then, when you take sums x + y, where x and y are in A, you can have:\n\nEven + Even = Even\n\nEven + Odd = Odd\n\nOdd + Even = Odd\n\nOdd + Odd = Even\n\nTherefore, the sumset S would consist of all even numbers from 0 + 0 = 0 up to 99 + 99 = 198, and all odd numbers from 0 + 1 = 1 up to 98 + 99 = 197. So the even numbers in S are 0, 2, 4, ..., 198 (100 numbers) and the odd numbers are 1, 3,