ait, 1.456 is approximately 1.456, which is indeed less than 1.5. Therefore, the maximum distance within each slab is ~1.456, which is less than 1.5, so in such a coloring, no two points of the same color are 1.5 apart. Hence, part b) is false.\n\nBut wait, wait, 1.456 is less than 1.5. So if we partition the cube into three such regions, each of diameter ~1.456, which is less than 1.5, then any two points in the same region are at most ~1.456 apart, which is less than 1.5. Hence, there are no two points in the same color at 1.5 or more apart. Therefore, the answer to part b) is \"No\".\n\nTherefore, the final answers are:\n\na) Yes\n\nb) No", "deepseek_solution": "**Step-by-Step Explanation and Answer:**\n\n**Problem Statement:**  \nWe color every point of a unit cube with one of three colors. Determine if it is always true that there exist two points of the same color with distance at least:  \na) 1.4  \nb) 1.5  \n\n---\n\n**Analysis for Part (a):**\n\n1. **Key Distances in a Unit Cube:**  \n   - Edge length = 1  \n   - Face diagonal = \u221a2 \u2248 1.414  \n   - Space diagonal = \u221a3 \u2248 1.732  \n\n2. **Pigeonhole Principle Application:**  \n   - The cube has 8 vertices. Using 3 colors, by the pigeonhole principle, at least \\( \\lceil 8/3 \\rceil = 3 \\) vertices must share the same color.  \n\n3. **Distance Between Colored Vertices:**  \n   - Among any 3 vertices in the cube, there must be at least two separated by at least a face diagonal (\u221a2 \u2248 1.414).  \n   - This is because three vertices on a face must include a diagonal pair (distance \u221a2), and non-coplanar triples also contain pairs at \u221a2 or greater.  \n\n4. **Conclusion for Part (a):**  \n   - Since 1.4 < \u221a2 \u2248 1.414, two vertices of the same color will always exist with distance \u2265 \u221a2 > 1.4.  \n   - **Answer:** Yes, it is always true.  \n\n---\n\n**Analysis for Part (b):**\n\n1. **Target Distance (1.5):**  \n   - 1.5 lies between the face diagonal (\u22481.414) and space diagonal (\u22481.732).  \n\n2. **Partitioning Strategy:**  \n   - Divide the unit cube into three axis-aligned rectangular slabs along the x-axis:  \n     - Slab 1: \\( 0 \\leq x \\leq 1/3 \\)  \n     - Slab 2: \\( 1/3 \\leq x \\leq 2/3 \\)  \n     - Slab 3: \\( 2/3 \\leq x \\leq 1 \\)  \n\n3. **Diameter of Each Slab:**  \n   - The maximum distance within a slab (e.g., between \\( (0,0,0) \\) and \\( (1/3,1,1) \\)) is:  \n     \\[\n     \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^2 + 1^2 + 1^2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{19}{9}} \\approx 1.456 < 1.5.\n     \\]  \n   - Thus, no two points within the same slab exceed 1.456 in distance.  \n\n4. **Coloring Implication:**  \n   - Assign each slab a distinct color. Since the diameter of each slab is <1.5, no two points of the same color are 1.5 apart.  \n\n5. **Conclusion for Part (b):**  \n   - A valid 3-coloring exists where no two points of the same color are \u22651.5 apart.  \n   - **Answer:** No, it is not always true.  \n\n---\n\n**Final Answers:**  \na) \\(\\boxed{\\text{Yes}}\\)  \nb) \\(\\boxed{\\text{No}}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Consider the unit cube, which has 8 vertices. Each point in this cube is to be colored using one of three colors. \n\n2. By the pigeonhole principle, because there are 8 vertices and only 3 colors, at least one color must be used on at least \\( \\lceil \\frac{8}{3} \\rceil = 3 \\) vertices.\n\n3. Having identified that there must be at least 3 vertices sharing the same color, consider any such set of three vertices. There must exist two vertices among these three that are not adjacent (i.e., they don't share an edge).\n\n4. The minimum distance between any two non-adjacent vertices of the cube can be calculated. For any such two vertices, the distance is at least the length of the face diagonal of the cube, which is \\( \\sqrt{2} \\). Now:\n\n\\[ \\sqrt{2} \\approx 1.414 \\]\n\nThus, the distance is greater than 1.4.\n\n   **Conclusion:** It is always true that within the unit cube, there are at least two points of the same color whose distance is at least 1.4. \n\n   Therefore, the answer to part (a) is \\(\\boxed{\\text{yes}}\\).\n\n5. To address part (b), we need to determine if every possible coloring inevitably contains two same-colored points at a distance of at least 1.5.\n\n6. We construct a specific coloring scheme to show that not every coloring scheme will inevitably lead to this situation:\n\n   - Divide the unit cube into three rectangular prisms by partitioning it with planes parallel to one face of the cube.\n   - Let each plane divide a face into three equal parts along one axis, yielding three distinct regions within the unit cube. Each of these regions has a base that is one-third of the original cube's face.\n\n7. Assign different colors to each of these three regions. According to the problem, the goal is to see if any two points in this configuration can be farther apart than 1.5 units:\n\n   - The maximum distance \\( d \\) between any two points within one of these regions is the body's diagonal of each rectangular prism region. Given that the base is \\( \\frac{1}{3} \\) of the cube's side and the height is the same as the side of the cube, the body diagonal of a rectangular prism (using the Pythagorean theorem) is calculated as follows:\n\n     \\[\n     d = \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^2 + \\left(1\\right)^2 + \\left(1\\right)^2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9} + 1 + 1} = \\sqrt{\\frac{19}{9}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{3}\n     \\]\n\n8. Now, compute the numerical value:\n\n   - Since \\((\\sqrt{19}) \\approx 4.36\\):\n\n     \\[\n     \\frac{4.36}{3} \\approx 1.45 < 1.5\n     \\]\n\n   This implies the diagonal length is indeed less than 1.5.\n\n   **Conclusion:** It is possible to color the cube such that no two points of the same color have a distance of 1.5 or greater.\n\n   Therefore, the answer to part (b) is \\(\\boxed{\\text{no}}\\).", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": nul