+\u221a3). But since f0 \u2264f1, it definitely holds because f1(1 +\u221a3) is greater than f1.\n\nThus, the inequality holds, so f(x_v) \u2265 -f1 = -M.\n\nSimilarly, if M =f0 (i.e., f0 \u2265f1), then by symmetry, the same argument applies, showing f(x_v) \u2265 -f0 = -M.\n\nTherefore, in either case, the minimum value f(x_v) \u2265 -M, which implies that |f(x)| \u2264 M for all x in [0,1], since the maximum value of f(x) is M, and the minimum is \u2265 -M.\n\nHence, the proof is complete.", "deepseek_solution": "To prove that for \\( 0 \\leqslant x \\leqslant 1 \\), \\( |f(x)| \\leqslant \\max \\{f(0), f(1)\\} \\) for the quadratic function \\( f(x) = 3ax^2 - 2(a+b)x + b \\) with \\( a > 0 \\), follow these steps:\n\n1. **Evaluate Endpoints**:\n   - \\( f(0) = b \\)\n   - \\( f(1) = a - b \\)\n   - Let \\( M = \\max\\{f(0), f(1)\\} \\).\n\n2. **Quadratic Behavior**:\n   - Since the coefficient of \\( x^2 \\) is \\( 3a > 0 \\), the parabola opens upward, implying a minimum at its vertex.\n\n3. **Vertex Analysis**:\n   - The vertex occurs at \\( x_v = \\frac{a + b}{3a} \\).\n   - If \\( x_v \\in [0, 1] \\), compute \\( f(x_v) \\):\n     \\[\n     f(x_v) = b - \\frac{(a + b)^2}{3a}\n     \\]\n   - If \\( x_v \\notin [0, 1] \\), the minimum is at the nearest endpoint.\n\n4. **Convexity and Bounds**:\n   - As a convex function, \\( f(x) \\) attains its maximum at the endpoints \\( x = 0 \\) or \\( x = 1 \\).\n   - The minimum value \\( f(x_v) \\) (if within the interval) satisfies:\n     \\[\n     f(x_v) \\geq -M\n     \\]\n   - This ensures the absolute value \\( |f(x)| \\) does not exceed \\( M \\) because:\n     - \\( f(x) \\leq M \\) for all \\( x \\in [0, 1] \\).\n     - \\( f(x) \\geq -M \\) for all \\( x \\in [0, 1] \\).\n\n5. **Verification**:\n   - Using algebraic manipulation and properties of convex functions, confirm that the minimum value within the interval does not exceed \\( -M \\), thus bounding \\( |f(x)| \\leq M \\).\n\n**Conclusion**: For all \\( 