2 + 8bc}{a^2}\n    \\]\n    Simplifying, we get:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{1}{x^2} = 1 + \\frac{8bc}{a^2}\n    \\]\n    Therefore,\n    \\[\n    \\frac{1}{x^2} - 1 = \\frac{8bc}{a^2}\n    \\]\n\n4. Similarly, for \\(y\\) and \\(z\\):\n    \\[\n    \\frac{1}{y^2} - 1 = \\frac{8ac}{b^2}\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    \\frac{1}{z^2} - 1 = \\frac{8ab}{c^2}\n    \\]\n\n5. Multiplying these three expressions together, we have:\n    \\[\n    \\left(\\frac{1}{x^2} - 1\\right) \\left(\\frac{1}{y^2} - 1\\right) \\left(\\frac{1}{z^2} - 1\\right) = 512\n    \\]\n\n6. Suppose \\(x + y + z < 1\\), then \\(0 < x < 1\\), \\(0 < y < 1\\), and \\(0 < z < 1\\).\n\n7. From above we know:\n    \\[\n    \\left(\\frac{1}{x^2} - 1\\right) \\left(\\frac{1}{y^2} - 1\\right) \\left(\\frac{1}{z^2} - 1\\right)\n    \\]\n\n    Rearranging, we obtain:\n    \n    \\[\n    \\frac{(1 - x^2)(1 - y^2)(1 - z^2)}{x^2 y^2 z^2}\n    \\]\n    \n    Recall:\n    \n    \\[\n    \\frac{\\prod \\left[(\\sum x)^2 - x^2\\right]}{x^2 y^2 z^2}\n    \\]\n    \n    By exploring the equality in the context of product:\n    \n    \\[\n    \\frac{\\prod[(y+z)(2x+y+z)]}{x^2 y^2 z^2} \\geq \\frac{\\prod\\left(2 \\sqrt{yz} \\cdot 4 \\sqrt[4]{x^2 yz}\\right)}{x^2 y^2 z^2} \\geq 512\n    \\]\n    \n    Simplifying the expression we realize:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{\\prod\\left(8 \\sqrt[4]{x^2 y^3 z^3}\\right)}{x^2 y^2 z^2} = 512\n    \\]\n\n8. This contradicts our assumption. Hence, \\(x + y + z \\geq 1\\).\n\n9. Therefore, for the minimum value of the expression, equality holds when \\(a = b = c\\). Substituting \\(a = b = c\\) gives us:\n    \\[\n    x = y = z = \\frac{a}{\\sqrt{a^2 + 8a^2}} = \\frac{a}{3a} = \\frac{1}{3}\n    \\]\n\nBy all calculated steps and proofs, the minimum value of the expression \\( \\frac{a}{\\sqrt{a^{2}+8bc}} + \\frac{b}{\\sqrt{b^{2}+8ac}} + \\frac{c}{\\sqrt{c^{2}+8ab}}\\) is \\(1\\). \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "\\[ \\operatorname{ctg} \\alpha - \\operatorname{tg} \\alpha - 2 \\operatorname{tg} 2 \\alpha - 4 \\operatorname{tg} 4 \\alpha = 8 \\operatorname{ctg} 8 \\alpha. \\]", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I have this trigonometric equation to prove: ctg \u03b1 - tg \u03b1 - 2 tg 2\u03b1 - 4 tg 4\u03b1 = 8 ctg 8\u03b1. Hmm, that's a bit intimidating at first glance. Let me try to break it down step by step. \n\nFirst, I know that ctg is the cotangent, which is 1/tg, right? So maybe expressing everything in terms of sine and cosine could help. Let me rewrite the equation using sine and cosine:\n\ncot \u03b1 - tan \u03b1 - 2 tan 2\u03b1 - 4 tan 4\u03b1 = 8 cot 8\u03b1.\n\nSo, in terms of sine and cosine, that would be:\n\n(cos \u03b1 / sin \u03b1) - (sin \u03b1 / cos \u03b1) - 2*(sin 2\u03b1 / cos 2\u03b1) - 4*(sin 4\u03b1 / cos 4\u03b1) = 8*(cos 8\u03b1 / sin 8\u03b1).\n\nHmm, not sure if this is the best approach. Maybe there's a way to use trigonometric identities to simplify the left side and see if it equals the right side. Let's recall some identities. \n\nI remember that cot \u03b1 - tan \u03b1 can be simplified. Let me compute that first:\n\ncot \u03b1 - tan \u03b1 = (cos \u03b1 / sin \u03b1) - (sin \u03b1 / cos \u03b1) = [cos\u00b2 \u03b1 - sin\u00b2 \u03b1] / (sin \u03b1 cos \u03b1). \n\nOh, wait, the numerator is cos 2\u03b1, because cos\u00b2 \u03b1 - sin\u00b2 \u03b1 = cos 2\u03b1. So that simplifies to:\n\ncos 2\u03b1 / (sin \u03b1 cos \u03b1).\n\nBut sin \u03b1 cos \u03b1 is (1/2) sin 2\u03b1. So that becomes:\n\ncos 2\u03b1 / ( (1/2) sin 2\u03b1 ) ) = 2 cos 2\u03b1 / sin 2\u03b1 = 2 cot 2\u03b1.\n\nSo, cot \u03b1 - tan \u03b1 = 2 cot 2\u03b1. Interesting! So that term simplifies to 2 cot 2\u03b1. Let me note that.\n\nSo substituting back into the original equation:\n\n2 cot 2\u03b1 - 2 tan 2\u03b1 - 4 tan 4\u03b1 = 8 cot 8\u03b1.\n\nWait, the original equation after substituting the first two terms becomes 2 cot 2\u03b1 - 2 tan 2\u03b1 - 4 tan 4\u03b1. Let me check again:\n\nOriginal left side: cot \u03b1 - tan \u03b1 - 2 tan 2\u03b1 - 4 tan 4\u03b1. \n\nAfter replacing cot \u03b1 - tan \u03b1 with 2 cot 2\u03b1, we get:\n\n2 cot 2\u03b1 - 2 tan 2\u03b1 - 4 tan 4\u03b1.\n\nHmm, perhaps I can factor out a 2 from the first two terms:\n\n2(cot 2\u03b1 - tan 2\u03b1) - 4 tan 4\u03b1.\n\nAgain, cot 2\u03b1 - tan 2\u03b1 might simplify. Let me compute that:\n\ncot 2\u03b1 - tan 2\u03b1 = (cos 2\u03b1 / sin 2\u03b1) - (sin 2\u03b1 / cos 2\u03b1) = [cos\u00b2 2\u03b1 - sin\u00b2 2\u03b1] / (sin 2\u03b1 cos 2\u03b1).\n\nThe numerator is cos 4\u03b1, since cos\u00b2 x - sin\u00b2 x = cos 2x. So here, x is 2\u03b1, so cos 4\u03b1. Therefore, numerator is cos 4\u03b1, denominator is (sin 2\u03b1 cos 2\u03b1) = (1/2) sin 4\u03b1. So:\n\ncos 4\u03b1 / ( (1/2) sin 4\u03b1 ) ) = 2 cos 4\u03b1 / sin 4\u03b1 = 2 cot 4\u03b1.\n\nTherefore, cot 2\u03b1 - tan 2\u03b1 = 2 cot 4\u03b1. Therefore, substituting back into the left side:\n\n2*(2 cot 4\u03b1) - 4 tan 4\u03b1 = 4 cot 4\u03b1 - 4 tan 4\u03b1.\n\nSo now the equation simplifies to:\n\n4 cot 4\u03b1 - 4 tan 4\u03b1 = 8 cot 8\u03b1.\n\nHmm, again, let's factor out 4:\n\n4*(cot 4\u03b1 - tan 4\u03b1) = 8 cot 8\u03b1.\n\nDivide both sides by 4:\n\ncot 4\u03b1 - tan 4\u03b1 = 2 cot 8\u03b1.\n\nAgain, let's check if cot 4\u03b1 - tan 4\u03b1 simplifies to 2 cot 8\u03b1. Let's compute cot 4\u03b1 - tan 4\u03b1:\n\ncot 4\u03b1 - tan 4\u03b1 = (cos 4\u03b1 / sin 4\u03b1) - (sin 4\u03b1 / cos 4\u03b1) = [cos\u00b2 4\u03b1 - sin\u00b2 4\u03b1] / (sin 4\u03b1 cos 4\u03b1).\n\nNumerator is cos 8\u03b1 (since cos 2x = cos\u00b2 x - sin\u00b2 x, so x = 4\u03b1 here). Denominator is (sin 4\u03b1 cos 4\u03b1) = (1/2) sin 8\u03b1. Therefore:\n\ncos 8\u03b1 / ( (1/2) sin 8\u03b1 ) = 2 cos 8\u03b1 / sin 8\u03b1 = 2 cot 8\u03b1.\n\nExactly! Therefore, cot 4\u03b1 - tan 4\u03b1 = 2 cot 8\u03b1. Therefore, substituting back:\n\n4*(2 cot 8\u03b1) = 8 cot 8\u03b1. \n\nWhich gives 8 cot 8\u03b1 = 8 cot 8\u03b1. Hence, the equation holds true. \n\nTherefore, starting from the left side, by repeatedly applying the identity cot x - tan x = 2 cot 2x, we can reduce the expression step by step until it matches the right side. \n\nSo, the key steps were recognizing that cot \u03b1 - tan \u03b1 simplifies to 2 cot 2\u03b1, then repeating this process for cot 2\u03b1 - tan 2\u03b1 to get 2 cot 4\u03b1, and so on, each time doubling the angle and halving the coefficients. This pattern continues until we reach 8 cot 8\u03b1, which matches the right side of the equation. \n\nI think this shows that the original equation is indeed an identity, valid for all \u03b1 where the functions are defined (i.e., where the denominators aren't zero). Let me just check with a sample angle to confirm. \n\nLet's pick \u03b1 = \u03c0/16. Then 8\u03b1 = \u03c0/2, so cot 8\u03b1 = cot(\u03c0/2) = 0. So the right side is 0. Let's compute the left side:\n\ncot(\u03c0/16) - tan(\u03c0/16) - 2 tan(\u03c0/8) - 4 tan(\u03c0/4).\n\nCompute each term:\n\ncot(\u03c0/16) \u2248 1 / tan(\u03c0/16) \u2248 1 / 0.1989 \u2248 5.027.\n\ntan(\u03c0/16) \u2248 0.1989.\n\ntan(\u03c0/8) \u2248 0.4142.\n\ntan(\u03c0/4) = 1.\n\nSo left side \u2248 5.027 - 0.1989 - 2*(0.4142) - 4*(1) \u2248 5.027 - 0.1989 - 0.8284 - 4 \u2248 5.027 - 5.027 \u2248 0. Which matches the right side. That checks out.\n\nAnother test: \u03b1 = \u03c0/24. Then 8\u03b1 = \u03c0/3, so cot 8\u03b1 = cot(\u03c0/3) = 1/\u221a3 \u2248 0.577. So right side is 8*(1/\u221a3) \u2248 4.618.\n\nLeft side:\n\ncot(\u03c0/24) - tan(\u03c0/24) - 2 tan(\u03c0/12) - 4 tan(\u03c0/6).\n\nCompute each term:\n\ncot(\u03c0/24) \u2248 1 / tan(\u03c0/24) \u2248 1 / 0.1317 \u2248 7.596.\n\ntan(\u03c0/24) \u2248 0.1317.\n\ntan(\u03c0/12) \u2248 0.2679.\n\ntan(\u03c0/6) \u2248 0.5774.\n\nLeft side \u2248 7.596 - 0.1317 - 2*(0.2679) - 4*(0.5774) \u2248 7.596 - 0.1317 - 0.5358 - 2.3096 \u2248 7.596 - 2.9771 \u2248 4.6189. Which is close to 4.618. Given rounding errors, that's a good approximation. So the identity holds here too.\n\nTherefore, through both algebraic manipulation using trigonometric identities and numerical verification, the equation is proven to be correct.", "deepseek_solution": "To prove the trigonometric identity:\n\\[\n\\operatorname{ctg} \\alpha - \\operatorname{tg} \\alpha - 2 \\operatorname{tg} 2\\alpha - 4 \\operatorname{tg} 4\\alpha = 8 \\operatorname{ctg} 8\\alpha,\n\\]\nwe proceed by systematically simplifying the left-hand side using trigonometric identities.\n\n**Step 1: Simplify \\(\\operatorname{ctg} \\alpha - \\operatorname{tg} \\alpha\\)**\n\\[\n\\operatorname{ctg} \\alpha - \\operatorname{tg} \\alpha = \\frac{\\cos \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha} - \\frac{\\sin \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha} = \\frac{\\cos^2 \\alpha - \\sin^2 \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha \\cos \\alpha} = \\frac{\\cos 2\\alpha}{\\frac{1}{2}\\sin 2\\alpha} = 2 \\operatorname{ctg} 2\\alpha.\n\\]\nThus, the left-hand side becomes:\n\\[\n2 \\operatorname{ctg} 2\\alpha - 2 \\operatorname{tg} 2\\alpha - 4 \\operatorname{tg} 4\\alpha.\n\\]\n\n**Step 2: Simplify \\(\\operatorname{ctg} 2\\alpha - \\operatorname{tg} 2\\alpha\\)**\n\\[\n\\operatorname{ctg} 2\\alpha - \\operatorname{tg} 2\\alpha = \\frac{\\cos 2\\alpha}{\\sin 2\\alpha} - \\frac{\\sin 2\\alpha}{\\cos 2\\alpha} = \\frac{\\cos^2 2\\alpha - \\sin^2 2\\alpha}{\\sin 2\\alpha \\cos 2\\alpha} = \\frac{\\cos 4\\alpha}{\\frac{1}{2}\\sin 4\\alpha} = 2 \\operatorname{ctg} 4\\alpha.\n\\]\nSubstituting back, the left-hand side becomes:\n\\[\n4 \\operatorname{ctg} 4\\alpha - 4 \\operatorname{tg} 4\\alpha.\n\\]\n\n**Step 3: Simplify \\(\\operatorname{ctg} 4\\alpha - \\operatorname{tg} 4\\alpha\\)**\n\\[\n\\operatorname{ctg} 4\\alpha - \\operatorname{tg} 4\\alpha = \\frac{\\cos 4\\alpha}{\\sin 4\\alpha} - \\frac{\\sin 4\\alpha}{\\cos 4\\alpha} = \\frac{\\cos^2 4\\alpha - \\sin^2 4\\alpha}{\\sin 4\\alpha \\cos 4\\alpha} = \\frac{\\cos 8\\alpha}{\\frac{1}{2}\\sin 8\\alpha} = 2 \\operatorname{ctg} 8\\alpha.\n\\]\nSubstituting back, the left-hand side simplifies to:\n\\[\n8 \\operatorname{ctg} 8\\alpha.\n\\]\n\n**Conclusion:**\nThe left-hand side simplifies step-by-step to match the right-hand side:\n\\[\n\\operatorname{ctg} \\alpha - \\operatorname{tg} \\alpha - 2 \\operatorname{tg} 2\\alpha - 4 \\operatorname{tg} 4\\alpha = 8 \\operatorname{ctg} 8\\alpha.\n\\]\nThis confirms the identity is valid for all \\(\\alpha\\) where the functions are defined.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{8 \\operatorname{ctg} 8\\alpha}", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Write the given equation in terms of trigonometric functions:\n\\[\n\\operatorname{ctg} \\alpha - \\operatorname{tg} \\alpha - 2 \\operatorname{tg} 2\\alpha - 4 \\operatorname{tg} 4\\alpha = 8 \\operatorname{ctg} 8\\alpha\n\\]\n\n2. Express $\\operatorname{ctg} \\alpha$ and $\\operatorname{tg} \\alpha$ using sine and cosine functions:\n\\[\n\\operatorname{ctg} \\alpha = \\frac{\\cos \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha}, \\quad \\operatorname{tg} \\alpha = \\frac{\\sin \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha}\n\\]\nThus, the left-hand side becomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\cos \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha} - \\frac{\\sin \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha} - 2 \\operatorname{tg} 2 \\alpha - 4 \\operatorname{tg} 4 \\alpha\n\\]\n\n3. Combine the first two terms using a common denominator:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\cos \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha} - \\frac{\\sin \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha} = \\frac{\\cos^2 \\alpha - \\sin^2 \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha \\cos \\alpha}\n\\]\n\n4. Recognize that $\\cos^2 \\alpha - \\sin^2 \\alpha = \\cos 2\\alpha$ and simplify:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\cos^2 \\alpha - \\sin^2 \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha \\cos \\alpha} = \\frac{\\cos 2\\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha \\cos \\alpha} = \\frac{2 \\cos 2\\alpha}{2 \\sin \\alpha \\cos \\alpha} = \\frac{2 \\cos 2\\alpha}{\\sin 2\\alpha} = 2 \\operatorname{ctg} 2\\alpha\n\\]\n\n5. The expression now reduces to:\n\\[\n2 \\operatorname{ctg} 2\\alpha - 2 \\operatorname{tg} 2\\alpha - 4 \\operatorname{tg} 4\\alpha\n\\]\n\n6. Factor out common terms:\n\\[\n2 (\\operatorname{ctg} 2\\alpha - \\operatorname{tg} 2\\alpha) - 4 \\operatorname{tg} 4\\alpha\n\\]\n\n7. Express $\\operatorname{ctg} 2\\alpha$ and $\\operatorname{tg} 2\\alpha$ similarly to step 2 and combine them:\n\\[\n2 \\left( \\frac{\\cos 2\\alpha}{\\sin 2\\alpha} - \\frac{\\sin 2\\alpha}{\\cos 2\\alpha} \\right) - 4 \\operatorname{tg} 4\\alpha\n\\]\n\\[\n2 \\left( \\frac{\\cos^2 2\\alpha - \\sin^2 2\\alpha}{\\sin 2\\alpha \\cos 2\\alpha} \\right) - 4 \\operatorname{tg} 4\\alpha\n\\]\n\n8. Recognize $\\cos^2 2\\alpha - \\sin^2 2\\alpha = \\cos 4\\alpha$ and simplify:\n\\[\n2 \\left( \\frac{\\cos^2 2\\alpha - \\sin^2 2\\alpha}{\\sin 2\\alpha \\cos 2\\alpha} \\right) = 2 \\left( \\frac{\\cos 4\\alpha}{\\sin 2\\alpha \\cos 2\\alpha} \\right)\n\\]\n\\[\n= 2 \\left( \\frac{2 \\cos 4\\alpha}{2 \\sin 2\\alpha \\cos 2\\alpha} \\right) = \\frac{4 \\cos 4\\alpha}{\\sin 4\\alpha} = 4 \\operatorname{ctg} 4\\alpha\n\\]\n\n9. Thus, we have:\n\\[\n4 \\operatorname{ctg} 4\\alpha - 4 \\operatorname{tg} 4\\alpha\n\\]\n\n10. Factor again:\n\\[\n4 \\left( \\operatorname{ctg} 4\\alpha - \\operatorname{tg} 4\\alpha \\right)\n\\]\n\n11. Combine and simplify terms as follows for $\\operatorname{ctg}$ and $\\operatorname{tg}$:\n\\[\n4 \\left( \\frac{\\cos 4\\alpha}{\\sin 4\\alpha} - \\frac{\\sin 4\\alpha}{\\cos 4\\alpha} \\right) = 4 \\left( \\frac{\\cos^2 4\\alpha - \\sin^2 4\\alpha}{\\sin 4\\alpha \\cos 4\\alpha} \\right)\n\\]\nRecognize $\\cos^2 4\\alpha - \\sin^2 4\\alpha = \\cos 8\\alpha$:\n\\[\n4 \\left( \\frac{2 \\cos^2 4\\alpha - 2 \\sin^2 4\\alpha}{2 \\sin 4\\alpha \\cos 4\\alpha} \\right) = 8 \\left( \\frac{\\cos 8\\alpha}{\\sin 8\\alpha} \\right)\n\\]\n\n12. The final result is:\n\\[\n8 \\operatorname{ctg} 8\\alpha = 8 \\operatorname{ctg} 8\\alpha\n\\]\n\nConclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{8 \\operatorname{ctg} 8\\alpha}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let \\( x, y, z \\in (0, 1) \\). Prove that \\( x(1-y)(1-z) + (1-x)y(1-z) + (1-x)(1-y)z < 