tant of integration \\(C\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n\\int \\sin 7x \\cos 3x \\, dx = -\\frac{\\cos 10x}{20} - \\frac{\\cos 4x}{8} + C\n\\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\dfrac{\\cos 10x}{20} - \\dfrac{\\cos 4x}{8} + C}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Identify the integral:**\n   \\[\n   \\int \\sin(7x) \\cos(3x) \\, dx\n   \\]\n\n2. **Use the product-to-sum identities:**\n   The product-to-sum identities in trigonometry allow us to transform the product of sines and cosines into a sum of sines or cosines. For \\(\\sin(Ax)\\cos(Bx)\\), we use:\n   \\[\n   \\sin(Ax)\\cos(Bx) = \\frac{1}{2}[\\sin((A+B)x) + \\sin((A-B)x)]\n   \\]\n   Setting \\(A = 7\\) and \\(B = 3\\), we get:\n   \\[\n   \\sin(7x) \\cos(3x) = \\frac{1}{2} [\\sin((7+3)x) + \\sin((7-3)x)] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\sin(10x) + \\sin(4x)]\n   \\]\n\n3. **Rewrite the integral:**\n   \\[\n   \\int \\sin(7x) \\cos(3x) \\, dx = \\int \\frac{1}{2}[\\sin(10x) + \\sin(4x)] \\, dx\n   \\]\n   Distribute the integral over the sum:\n   \\[\n   \\int \\frac{1}{2} [\\sin(10x) + \\sin(4x)] \\, dx = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\int \\sin(10x) \\, dx + \\int \\sin(4x) \\, dx \\right)\n   \\]\n\n4. **Integrate each term separately:**\n   For \\(\\int \\sin(10x) \\, dx\\):\n   \\[\n   \\int \\sin(10x) \\, dx = -\\frac{1}{10} \\cos(10x) + C_1\n   \\]\n   For \\(\\int \\sin(4x) \\, dx\\):\n   \\[\n   \\int \\sin(4x) \\, dx = -\\frac{1}{4} \\cos(4x) + C_2\n   \\]\n\n5. **Combine the results:**\n   Substitute these results back into the original integral:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{2} \\left( -\\frac{1}{10} \\cos(10x) + C_1 + -\\frac{1}{4} \\cos(4x) + C_2 \\right)\n   \\]\n\n6. **Simplify the constants and final result:**\n   Let \\(C = \\frac{1}{2}(C_1 + C_2)\\) be a single constant of integration:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{2} \\left( -\\frac{1}{10} \\cos(10x) - \\frac{1}{4} \\cos(4x) \\right) + C\n   \\]\n   Combining terms inside:\n   \\[\n   -\\frac{1}{20} \\cos(10x) - \\frac{1}{8} \\cos(4x) + C\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\bo