e car. When he launches at time t, the car will travel in the direction \u03b8(t) = \u03c0 t at 1 m/s for the remaining time (1 - t) seconds. Therefore, the displacement vector from the center is (1 - t) * (cos(\u03c0 t), sin(\u03c0 t)). So, the position is ( (1 - t) cos \u03c0 t, (1 - t) sin \u03c0 t ).\n\nTherefore, the set S is the set of all such points for t in [0, 1]. So, S is the trajectory of the parametric curve ( (1 - t) cos \u03c0 t, (1 - t) sin \u03c0 t ) as t goes from 0 to 1. Then, the area of S is the area covered by all these points. Wait, but S is a continuous set of points, so it's a curve, but the problem says \"the set S of points the mouse can reach in his car within 1 second\". But since the mouse can choose any t in [0,1], and for each t, he reaches a specific point. So S is the image of the parametric curve from t=0 to t=1. But the area of a curve is zero, unless it's an area covered by all possible positions. Wait, perhaps not. Wait, no, the car leaves the turntable at time t, and then travels for (1 - t) seconds. So the position at time 1 second after the turntable starts is the point where the car is when it stops. Wait, but does the car stop automatically after 1 second? The problem says \"within 1 second after the turntable is set in motion\". So the mouse can choose to launch the car at any time t between 0 and 1, and once launched, the car goes straight at 1 m/s. The car will keep moving indefinitely unless the mouse stops it. But the problem says \"within 1 second\", so perhaps the mouse must stop the car before or at 1 second. Therefore, the maximum time the car can be moving is 1 second. But if the mouse launches at time t, the car can only move until time 1, so the duration is (1 - t) seconds. Therefore, the displacement is 1*(1 - t) meters in the direction determined by the turntable's angle at time t. Therefore, the position at time 1 is ( (1 - t) cos \u03b8(t), (1 - t) sin \u03b8(t) ), where \u03b8(t) = \u03c0 t, as established 