Mouse's travel:**\n   The mouse has a time limit of \\( 1 \\) second to reach any point. If the mouse waits for some time while the turntable rotates through an angle \\( \\theta \\), it can then launch the car along that direction. Given the car's speed of \\( 1 \\, \\text{m/s} \\), the mouse can travel up to \\( 1 \\, \\text{meter} \\) from its initial position.\n\n3. **Distance reached:**\n   If the mouse waits for an angle \\( \\theta \\), the remaining time to travel will be \\( (1 - \\frac{\\theta}{\\pi} \\, \\text{seconds}) \\). Thus, he covers a linear distance of:\n   \\[\n   r = 1 - \\frac{\\theta}{\\pi}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Polar coordinates formulation:**\n   The mouse can wait for any \\( \\theta \\) from \\( 0 \\) to \\( \\pi \\) radians before launching. The maximum radial distance \\( r \\) he can move along each direction is thus \\( 1 - \\frac{\\theta}{\\pi} \\).\n\n5. **Area computation using polar coordinates:**\n   The area \\( A \\) enclosed by this range can be given by a polar integral:\n   \\[\n   A = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\frac{(1 - \\frac{\\theta}{\\pi})^2}{2} d\\theta\n   \\]\n\n6. **Integrating:**\n   To solve this integral, we perform a substitution \\( \\phi = \\pi - \\theta \\):\n   \\[\n   d\\theta = -d\\phi \\quad \\text{and when} \\quad \\theta = 0, \\, \\phi = \\pi \\quad \\text{and when} \\quad \\theta = \\pi, \\, \\phi = 0\n   \\]\n\n   Inverting limits, we get:\n   \\[\n   A = \\int_{\\pi}^{0} \\frac{(1 - \\frac{\\pi - \\phi}{\\pi})^2}{2} (-d\\phi) = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\frac{\\phi^2}{2\\pi^2} d\\phi\n   \\]\n\n7. **Simplify and integrate:**\n   \\[\n   \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\frac{\\phi^2}{2\\pi^2} d\\phi = \\frac{1}{2\\pi^2} \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\phi^2 d\\phi\n   \\]\n\n   Evaluating integral \\( \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\phi^2 d\\phi \\):\n   \\[\n   \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\phi^2 d\\phi = \\left[ \\frac{\\phi^3}{3} \\right]_{0}^{\\pi} = \\frac{\\pi^3}{3}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Final computation:**\n   Thus,\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{2\\pi^2} \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\phi^2