\u03a3n(n-1)x^{n-2} = x\u00b2 d\u00b2/dx\u00b2 \u03a3x^n = x\u00b2 * 2/(1 - x)^3\n\n\u800c\u03a3n x^n = x/(1 - x)^2\n\n\u56e0\u6b64\uff0c\u5c06\u8fd9\u4e9b\u4ee3\u5165\uff1a\n\n\u03a3n\u00b3x^n = x\u00b3*6/(1 - x)^4 + 3*x\u00b2*2/(1 - x)^3 + x/(1 - x)^2\n\n= 6x\u00b3/(1 - x)^4 + 6x\u00b2/(1 - x)^3 + x/(1 - x)^2\n\n\u73b0\u5728\uff0c\u5c06\u8fd9\u4e9b\u901a\u5206\u5230\u540c\u4e00\u4e2a\u5206\u6bcd\uff1a\n\n\u9996\u5148\uff0c6x\u00b3/(1 - x)^4 + 6x\u00b2/(1 - x)^3 = 6x\u00b3/(1 - x)^4 + 6x\u00b2(1 - x)/(1 - x)^4 = [6x\u00b3 +6x\u00b2(1 - x)]/(1 - x)^4 = [6x\u00b3 +6x\u00b2 -6x\u00b3]/(1 - x)^4 = 6x\u00b2/(1 - x)^4\n\n\u7136\u540e\u518d\u52a0\u4e0ax/(1 - x)^2 = x(1 - x)^2/(1 - x)^4 = x(1 - 2x +x\u00b2)/(1 - x)^4\n\n\u6240\u4ee5\u603b\u548c\u4e3a\uff1a\n\n6x\u00b2/(1 - x)^4 + x(1 - 2x +x\u00b2)/(1 - x)^4 = [6x\u00b2 +x -2x\u00b2 +x\u00b3]/(1 - x)^4 = [x\u00b3 +4x\u00b2 +x]/(1 - x)^4 = x(x\u00b2 +4x +1)/(1 - x)^4\n\n\u8fd9\u548c\u4e4b\u524d\u7684\u7ed3\u679c\u4e00\u81f4\uff0c\u8bf4\u660e\u8fd9\u4e2a\u516c\u5f0f\u662f\u6b63\u786e\u7684\u3002\u56e0\u6b64\uff0c\u5e26\u5165x=1/3\u5f97\u5230\u7684\u7ed3\u679c33/8\u662f\u6b63\u786e\u7684\u3002\n\n\u4e0d\u8fc7\uff0c\u518d\u68c0\u67e5\u4e00\u904d\u4ee3\u5165\u8ba1\u7b97\u662f\u5426\u6b63\u786e\uff1a\n\nx=1/3\u4ee3\u5165\u5206\u5b50x(x\u00b2 +4x +1)= (1/3)( (1/3)^2 +4*(1/3) +1 ) = (1/3)(1/9 +4/3 +1) = (1/3)(1/9 +12/9 +9/9) = (1/3)(22/9)=22/27\n\n\u5206\u6bcd\u662f(1 -x)^4=(2/3)^4=16/81\n\n\u6240\u4ee5\u6574\u4f53\u662f22/27 \u00f7 16/81 =22/27 *81/16= (22*3)/16=66/16=33/8\uff0c\u6ca1\u9519\u3002\n\n\u6240\u4ee5\u8fd9\u4e2a\u7b54\u6848\u662f\u5bf9\u7684\uff0c\u5c31\u662f33/8\u3002\u6240\u4ee5\u539f\u6765\u7684\u65e0\u9650\u7ea7\u6570\u7684\u548c\u662f33/8\uff0c\u53734\u53c81/8\u3002\n\n\u4e0d\u8fc7\uff0c\u4e3a\u4e86\u5f7b\u5e95\u786e\u8ba4\uff0c\u6211\u53ef\u4ee5\u8ba1\u7b97\u524d\u51e0\u9879\u7684\u548c\uff0c\u770b\u770b\u662f\u5426\u63a5\u8fd1\u8fd9\u4e2a\u503c\u3002\n\n\u6bd4\u5982\u8ba1\u7b97\u524d\u56db\u9879\uff1a\n\n1\u00b3/3\u00b9 = 1/3 \u22480.3333\n\n2\u00b3/3\u00b2=8/9\u22480.8889\uff0c\u7d2f\u79ef\u548c\u22481.2222\n\n3\u00b3/3\u00b3=27/27=1\uff0c\u7d2f\u79ef\u548c\u22482.2222\n\n4\u00b3/3\u2074=64/81\u22480.7901\uff0c\u7d2f\u79ef\u548c\u22483.0123\n\n\u7b2c\u4e94\u9879\uff1a5\u00b3/3\u2075=125/243\u22480.5144\uff0c\u7d2f\u79ef\u22483.5267\n\n\u7b2c\u516d\u9879\uff1a6\u00b3/3\u2076=216/729\u22480.2963\uff0c\u7d2f\u79ef\u22483.8230\n\n\u7b2c\u4e03\u9879\uff1a7\u00b3/3\u2077\u2248343/2187\u22480.1568\uff0c\u7d2f\u79ef\u22483.9798\n\n\u7b2c\u516b\u9879\uff1a8\u00b3/3\u2078\u2248512/6561\u22480.0780\uff0c\u7d2f\u79ef\u22484.0578\n\n\u7b2c\u4e5d\u9879\uff1a9\u00b3/3\u2079\u2248729/19683\u22480.0370\uff0c\u7d2f\u79ef\u22484.0948\n\n\u7b2c\u5341\u9879\uff1a10\u00b3/3\u00b9\u2070\u22481000/59049\u22480.0169\uff0c\u7d2f\u79ef\u22484.1117\n\n\u7ee7\u7eed\u8ba1\u7b97\u66f4\u591a\u7684\u9879\uff0c\u4f1a\u53d1\u73b0\u9010\u6e10\u8d8b\u8fd1\u4e8e33/8=4.125\uff0c\u6bd4\u5982\u5230\u7b2c15\u9879\uff1a\n\n15\u00b3/3\u00b9\u2075\u22483375/14348907\u22480.000235\uff0c\u6240\u4ee5\u7d2f\u79ef\u548c\u4f1a\u8d8a\u6765\u8d8a\u63a5\u8fd14.125\u3002\u8fd9\u8bf4\u660e\u7ed3\u679c\u5408\u7406\u3002\n\n\u7efc\u4e0a\u6240\u8ff0\uff0c\u8fd9\u4e2a\u65e0\u9650\u7ea7\u6570\u7684\u548c\u662f33/8\u3002", "deepseek_solution": "\u8981\u8ba1\u7b97\u65e0\u7a77\u7ea7\u6570 \\(\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{n^3}{3^n}\\) \u7684\u548c\uff0c\u6211\u4eec\u53ef\u4ee5\u5229\u7528\u751f\u6210\u51fd\u6570\u548c\u6c42\u5bfc\u7684\u65b9\u6cd5\u3002\u4ee5\u4e0b\u662f\u8be6\u7ec6\u7684\u63a8\u5bfc\u8fc7\u7a0b\uff1a\n\n1. **\u751f\u6210\u51fd\u6570\u4e0e\u6c42\u5bfc\u6cd5**  \n   \u5df2\u77e5\u5bf9\u4e8e \\(|x| < 1\\)\uff0c\u51e0\u4f55\u7ea7\u6570 \\(\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} x^n = \\frac{1}{1-x}\\)\u3002\u901a\u8fc7\u5bf9\u8be5\u7ea7\u6570\u9010\u6b21\u6c42\u5bfc\u5e76\u6574\u7406\uff0c\u53ef\u5f97\uff1a\n   - \\(\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} n x^n = \\frac{x}{(1-x)^2}\\)\n   - \\(\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} n^2 x^n = \\frac{x(1+x)}{(1-x)^3}\\)\n   - \\(\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} n^3 x^n = \\frac{x(1 + 4x + x^2)}{(1-x)^4}\\)\n\n2. **\u4ee3\u5165 \\(x = \\frac{1}{3}\\)**  \n   \u5c06 \\(x = \\frac{1}{3}\\) \u4ee3\u5165\u4e0a\u8ff0\u4e09\u6b21\u5e42\u7ea7\u6570\u7684\u8868\u8fbe\u5f0f\uff1a\n   \\[\n   S = \\frac{\\frac{1}{3} \\left(1 + 4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} + \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^2\\right)}{\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{3}\\right)^4}\n   \\]\n\n3. **\u5206\u5b50\u548c\u5206\u6bcd\u7684\u8ba1\u7b97**  \n   - **\u5206\u5b50**\uff1a  \n     \\[\n     1 + \\frac{4}{3} + \\frac{1}{9} = \\frac{9}{9} + \\frac{12}{9} + \\frac{1}{9} = \\frac{22}{9}\n     \\]\n     \u56e0\u6b64\uff0c\u5206\u5b50\u90e8\u5206\u4e3a \\(\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\frac{22}{9} = \\frac{22}{27}\\)\u3002\n\n   - **\u5206\u6bcd**\uff1a  \n     \\[\n     \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^4 = \\frac{16}{81}\n     \\]\n\n4. **\u6700\u7ec8\u7ed3\u679c**  \n   \u5c06\u5206\u5b50\u548c\u5206\u6bcd\u4ee3\u5165\uff1a\n   \\[\n   S = \\frac{\\frac{22}{27}}{\\frac{16}{81}} = \\frac{22}{27} \\cdot \\frac{81}{16} = \\frac{22 \\cdot 3}{16} = \\frac{66}{16} = \\frac{33}{8}\n   \\]\n\n**\u7b54\u6848**  \n\u8be5\u65e0\u9650\u7ea7\u6570\u7684\u548c\u4e3a \\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{33}{8}}\\)\u3002", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Define \\( S \\) as the sum of the infinite series:\n\n   \\[\n   S = \\frac{1^3}{3^1} + \\frac{2^3}{3^2} + \\frac{3^3}{3^3} + \\frac{4^3}{3^4} + \\cdots\n   \\]\n\n2. Consider the series \\(\\frac{S}{3}\\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{S}{3} = \\frac{1^3}{3^2} + \\frac{2^3}{3^3} + \\frac{3^3}{3^4} + \\frac{4^3}{3^5} + \\cdots\n   \\]\n\n3. Subtract \\(\\frac{S}{3}\\) from \\(S\\):\n\n   \\[\n   S - \\frac{S}{3} = \\left(\\frac{1^3}{3^1} + \\frac{2^3}{3^2} + \\frac{3^3}{3^3} + \\frac{4^3}{3^4} + \\cdots \\right) - \\left(\\frac{1^3}{3^2} + \\frac{2^3}{3^3} + \\frac{3^3}{3^4} + \\frac{4^3}{3^5} + \\cdots \\right)\n   \\]\n\n4. Simplify the resulting series:\n\n   \\[\n   S - \\frac{S}{3} = \\frac{1^3}{3^1} + \\left(\\frac{2^3}{3^2} - \\frac{1^3}{3^2}\\right) + \\left(\\frac{3^3}{3^3} - \\frac{2^3}{3^3}\\right) + \\left(\\frac{4^3}{3^4} - \\frac{3^3}{3^4}\\right) + \\cdots\n   \\]\n\n5. Recognize that \\( n^3 - (n-1)^3 = 3n^2 - 3n + 1 \\). Therefore, each term in the series becomes:\n\n   \\[\n   S - \\frac{S}{3} = \\frac{1^3}{3^1} + \\frac{7}{3^2} + \\frac{19}{3^3} + \\frac{37}{3^4} + \\cdots\n   \\]\n\n6. Divide both sides of the equation by 3 to form a new series:\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{2S}{3} = \\frac{1}{9} + \\frac{2^3 - 1^3}{3^3} + \\frac{3^3 - 2^3}{3^4} + \\frac{4^3 - 3^3}{3^5} + \\cdots\n   \\]\n\n7. Subtract this new series from the original simplified series:\n\n   \\[\n   S - \\frac{2S}{3} = 1 + \\frac{12}{27} + \\frac{18}{81} + \\cdots\n   \\]\n\n8. Recognize that the remaining series is a geometric series where each term decreases exponentially with a common ratio of \\(\\frac{1}{3}\\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{S}{3} = \\frac{10}{9} + \\left( 6 \\times \\left( \\frac{1}{3^4} + \\frac{1}{3^5} + \\frac{1}{3^6} + \\cdots \\right) \\right)\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   = \\frac{10}{9} + 6 \\cdot \\frac{\\frac{1}{3^4}}{1 - \\frac{1}{3}}\n   \\]\n\n9. Simplify the infinite geometric series:\n\n   \\[\n   6 \\times \\frac{\\frac{1}{3^4}}{\\frac{2}{3}} = 6 \\times \\frac{1}{3^4} \\times \\frac{3}{2} = 6 \\times \\frac{1}{81} \\times \\frac{3}{2} = \\frac{1}{9}\n   \\]\n\n10. Combine the results:\n\n    \\[\n    \\frac{S}{3} = \\frac{10}{9} + \\frac{1}{9} = \\frac{11}{9}\n    \\]\n\n11. Solve for \\( S \\):\n\n    \\[\n    S = \\frac{33}{8}\n    \\]\n\nConclusion. The sum of the infinite series is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{33}{8}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "For the coefficients \\(a, b, c,\\) and \\(d\\) of two quadratic polynomials \\(x^{2} + b x + c\\) and \\(x^{2} + a x + d\\), it is known that \\(0 < a < b < c < d\\). Can these polynomials have a common root?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so the problem is asking if two quadratic polynomials, specifically x\u00b2 + b x + c and x\u00b2 + a x + d, can have a common root given that the coefficients satisfy 0 < a < b < c < d. Hmm, interesting. Let me try to figure this out step by step.\n\nFirst, let's recall what it means for two polynomials to have a common root. If there's a common root, say \u03b1, then \u03b1 must satisfy both equations simultaneously. So, substituting \u03b1 into both polynomials should give zero. That gives us two equations:\n\n1. \u03b1\u00b2 + b \u03b1 + c = 0\n2. \u03b1\u00b2 + a \u03b1 + d = 0\n\nIf we subtract the second equation from the first, we can eliminate \u03b1\u00b2. Let's do that:\n\n(\u03b1\u00b2 + b \u03b1 + c) - (\u03b1\u00b2 + a \u03b1 + d) = 0 - 0  \nSimplifying this gives: (b - a)\u03b1 + (c - d) = 0\n\nSo, (b - a)\u03b1 = d - c  \nTherefore, \u03b1 = (d - c)/(b - a)\n\nOkay, so if there's a common root, it has to be \u03b1 = (d - c)/(b - a). Now, since we know that 0 < a < b < c < d, let's look at the numerator and denominator here.\n\nThe denominator is (b - a). Since a and b are positive and a < b, this denominator is positive. The numerator is (d - c). Since c < d, this is also positive. So \u03b1 is a positive number. That's okay; roots can be positive or negative, but here it's positive.\n\nBut we need to check if this \u03b1 actually satisfies the original quadratic equations. Let's plug this \u03b1 back into one of the equations, say the first one: \u03b1\u00b2 + b \u03b1 + c = 0.\n\nSubstituting \u03b1:\n\n[(d - c)/(b - a)]\u00b2 + b*(d - c)/(b - a) + c = 0\n\nLet me compute this step by step. Let's denote \u03b1 = (d - c)/(b - a) for simplicity.\n\nFirst term: \u03b1\u00b2 = (d - c)\u00b2 / (b - a)\u00b2  \nSecond term: b \u03b1 = b*(d - c)/(b - a)  \nThird term: c\n\nSo adding them all together:\n\n[(d - c)\u00b2 + b(d - c)(b - a) + c(b - a)\u00b2] / (b - a)\u00b2 = 0\n\nSince the denominator can't be zero (because b > a), the numerator must be zero:\n\n(d - c)\u00b2 + b(d - c)(b - a) + c(b - a)\u00b2 = 0\n\nHmm, this equation needs to hold true for the coefficients a, b, c, d with 0 < a < b < c < d. Let's see if this is possible.\n\nLet me expand this numerator to see if it can simplify:\n\nFirst term: (d - c)\u00b2 = d\u00b2 - 2cd + c\u00b2  \nSecond term: b(d - c)(b - a) = b(d - c)(b - a) = b(b - a)(d - c)  \nThird term: c(b - a)\u00b2 = c(b\u00b2 - 2ab + a\u00b2)\n\nSo combining all these:\n\nd\u00b2 - 2cd + c\u00b2 + b(b - a)(d - c) + c(b\u00b2 - 2ab + a\u00b2) = 0\n\nThis looks complicated. Let's try to collect like terms. Let me compute each part:\n\nFirst term: d\u00b2 - 2cd + c\u00b2  \nSecond term: b(b - a)d - b(b - a)c  \nThird term: c b\u00b2 - 2ab c + c a\u00b2\n\nNow, let's write all terms:\n\n1. d\u00b2  \n2. -2cd  \n3. c\u00b2  \n4. b(b - a)d  \n5. -b(b - a)c  \n6. c b\u00b2  \n7. -2ab c  \n8. c a\u00b2\n\nLet me group terms with d\u00b2, terms with d, terms with c\u00b2, and constants.\n\nFirst, the d\u00b2 term:  \n1. d\u00b2\n\nTerms with d:  \n4. b(b - a)d  \n2. -2cd  \nSo d[b(b - a) - 2c]\n\nTerms with c\u00b2:  \n3. c\u00b2  \n5. -b(b - a)c  \n6. c b\u00b2  \n7. -2ab c  \n8. c a\u00b2\n\nWait, actually, terms 5, 6, 7, 8 are all terms involving c, but perhaps higher degree. Let me check again.\n\nWait, maybe I need to expand term 5: -b(b - a)c = -b\u00b2 c + a b c  \nTerm 6: c b\u00b2  \nTerm 7: -2 a b c  \nTerm 8: c a\u00b2  \nTerm 3: c\u00b2\n\nSo combining terms 5 and 6: -b\u00b2 c + a b c + c b\u00b2 = a b c  \nTerm 7: -2 a b c  \nTerm 8: c a\u00b2  \nTerm 3: c\u00b2\n\nSo combining 5,6,7,8,3: a b c -2 a b c + c a\u00b2 + c\u00b2  \n= -a b c + a\u00b2 c + c\u00b2  \n= c(-a b + a\u00b2 + c)\n\nSo putting all together:\n\nd\u00b2 + d[b(b - a) - 2c] + c(-a b + a\u00b2 + c) = 0\n\nSo overall:\n\nd\u00b2 + [b\u00b2 - a b - 2c] d + (-a b c + a\u00b2 c + c\u00b2) = 0\n\nHmm. This is a quadratic in d. Let me write it as:\n\nd\u00b2 + [b\u00b2 - a b - 2c] d + (c\u00b2 + a\u00b2 c - a b c) = 0\n\nThis quadratic equation must hold for some d > c (since d > c). Let's consider whether such a d exists.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can check if this equation can be satisfied given the constraints on the coefficients. Let's see.\n\nBut perhaps there is a smarter way. Let's think about the original equations.\n\nWe have two quadratics:\n\n1. x\u00b2 + b x + c = 0  \n2. x\u00b2 + a x + d = 0\n\nSuppose they have a common root \u03b1. Then, as we found, \u03b1 = (d - c)/(b - a). Let's also note that in quadratic equations, the sum and product of roots relate to coefficients.\n\nFor the first quadratic, the roots are \u03b1 and \u03b2, such that:\n\n\u03b1 + \u03b2 = -b  \n\u03b1 \u03b2 = c\n\nFor the second quadratic, the roots are \u03b1 and \u03b3, such that:\n\n\u03b1 + \u03b3 = -a  \n\u03b1 \u03b3 = d\n\nSo from the first equation, \u03b2 = -b - \u03b1  \nFrom the second equation, \u03b3 = -a - \u03b1\n\nWe know that the products are c and d respectively:\n\n\u03b1 \u03b2 = c  \n\u03b1 \u03b3 = d\n\nSo substituting \u03b2 and \u03b3:\n\n\u03b1 (-b - \u03b1) = c  \n\u03b1 (-a - \u03b1) = d\n\nSo:\n\n- b \u03b1 - \u03b1\u00b2 = c  \n- a \u03b1 - \u03b1\u00b2 = d\n\nLet's rewrite these equations:\n\nFrom the first equation: \u03b1\u00b2 + b \u03b1 + c = 0  \nFrom the second equation: \u03b1\u00b2 + a \u03b1 + d = 0\n\nWhich is consistent with our original equations.\n\nBut we can also express d in terms of \u03b1. From the second equation: d = -a \u03b1 - \u03b1\u00b2  \nSimilarly, from the first equation: c = -b \u03b1 - \u03b1\u00b2\n\nSo substituting c into d:\n\nd = -a \u03b1 - \u03b1\u00b2  \nBut c = -b \u03b1 - \u03b1\u00b2, so d = c + ( -a \u03b1 - \u03b1\u00b2 ) - ( -b \u03b1 - \u03b1\u00b2 ) ?\n\nWait, maybe not. Let me think. If we have expressions for c and d in terms of \u03b1, perhaps we can relate them.\n\nFrom c = -b \u03b1 - \u03b1\u00b2  \nFrom d = -a \u03b1 - \u03b1\u00b2\n\nTherefore, d - c = (-a \u03b1 - \u03b1\u00b2) - (-b \u03b1 - \u03b1\u00b2) = (-a \u03b1 + b \u03b1) = (b - a) \u03b1\n\nWhich gives us back \u03b1 = (d - c)/(b - a), as before. So that's consistent.\n\nBut since d > c, and b > a, this gives us a positive \u03b1 as we saw earlier.\n\nBut we need to ensure that c and d are positive as given. Wait, but c and d are coefficients of the quadratics, which are given to be positive (since 0 < a < b < c < d). However, in the expressions c = -b \u03b1 - \u03b1\u00b2 and d = -a \u03b1 - \u03b1\u00b2, the right-hand sides must also be positive.\n\nBut here's a problem: if \u03b1 is positive, then -b \u03b1 - \u03b1\u00b2 is negative, since both b and \u03b1 are positive. Similarly, -a \u03b1 - \u03b1\u00b2 is negative. But c and d are given as positive. This is a contradiction.\n\nWait, hold on. If \u03b1 is a root of x\u00b2 + b x + c = 0, then the product of the roots is c, which is positive. So both roots must be either both positive or both negative. Similarly, for the other quadratic, the product of the roots is d, which is positive, so both roots must be both positive or both negative.\n\nBut given that the coefficients of x (which are b and a) are positive, the sum of the roots for the first quadratic is -b, so the sum of roots is negative. Similarly, for the second quadratic, the sum of roots is -a, which is negative. Therefore, both quadratics must have two negative roots. Therefore, any common root \u03b1 must be negative. But earlier, we found \u03b1 = (d - c)/(b - a), which is positive because d > c and b > a. This is a contradiction. Therefore, such a common root cannot exist.\n\nWait, that seems like a key insight. Let me recap:\n\nIf a quadratic with positive coefficients (since a, b, c, d are all positive) of the form x\u00b2 + px + q has roots, then the sum of the roots is -p, which is negative, and the product is q, which is positive. Therefore, both roots must be negative. Therefore, any common root must be negative. However, we derived that \u03b1 = (d - c)/(b - a) which is positive because d > c and b > a. So \u03b1 must be positive, but the roots must be negative. Contradiction. Therefore, such a common root cannot exist.\n\nTherefore, the answer is no, these polynomials cannot have a common root.\n\nBut let me verify this reasoning once more to ensure there's no mistake.\n\nGiven that both quadratics have positive coefficients for x and the constant term, then by Vieta's formulas, their roots must both be negative. Hence, any common root would have to be negative. However, the value \u03b1 = (d - c)/(b - a) is positive because d > c and b > a. Therefore, this \u03b1 is positive, which can't be a root of either quadratic. Therefore, there is a contradiction, meaning such a common root cannot exist.\n\nTherefore, the answer is no.\n\nAlternatively, let's suppose for contradiction that there exists a common root \u03b1. Then \u03b1 must be negative. But we have \u03b1 = (d - c)/(b - a) > 0, which is impossible. Therefore, no such common root exists.\n\nYes, that seems solid. So the conclusion is that they cannot have a common root.", "deepseek_solution": "The two quadratic polynomials \\(x^{2} + b x + c\\) and \\(x^{2} + a x + d\\) with coefficients satisfying \\(0 < a < b < c < d\\) **cannot have a common root**. Here's the step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. **Common Root Condition**: If a common root \\(\\alpha\\) exists, it must satisfy both equations:\n   \\[\n   \\alpha^{2} + b \\alpha + c = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\alpha^{2} + a \\alpha + d = 0.\n   \\]\n   Subtracting these equations gives:\n   \\[\n   (b - a)\\alpha + (c - d) = 0 \\implies \\alpha = \\frac{d - c}{b - a}.\n   \\]\n   Since \\(d > c\\) and \\(b > a\\), \\(\\alpha > 0\\).\n\n2. **Vieta's Formulas**: For the quadratic \\(x^{2} + b x + c\\), the sum of roots is \\(-b\\) (negative), and the product is \\(c\\) (positive). Thus, **both roots must be negative**. Similarly, for \\(x^{2} + a x + d\\), the roots are also negative. Hence, any common root \\(\\alpha\\) must be **negative**.\n\n3. **Contradiction**: The derived value \\(\\alpha = \\frac{d - c}{b - a}\\) is **positive**, conflicting with the requirement that all roots of both quadratics are negative. This contradiction implies no such common root exists.\n\n**Final Answer**: \\(\\boxed{\\text{No}}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Given:**\n   - Two quadratic trinomials: \\( x^2 + bx + c \\) and \\( x^2 + ax + d \\).\n   - Coefficients \\( a, b, c, d \\) such that \\( 0 < a < b < c < d \\).\n   \n2. **Objective:**\n   - Determine if these trinomials can have a common root.\n   \n3. **Analysis:**\n   - Consider Vieta's formulas for roots of a quadratic trinomial \\( x^2 + px + q = 0 \\):\n     - Sum of the roots: \\( -p \\)\n     - Product of the roots: \\( q \\)\n   - Given positive coefficients, the sum and product of any roots of the given trinomials must fit within these bounds.\n\n4. **Assumption for contradiction:**\n   - Assume \\( x_0 \\) is a common root for the trinomials \\( x^2 + bx + c = 0 \\) and \\( x^2 + ax + d = 0 \\).\n\n5. **Step-by-step solution:**\n   - Since \\( x_0 \\) is a root for both, we have:\n     \\[\n     x_0^2 + bx_0 + c = 0 \n     \\]\n     \\[\n     x_0^2 + ax_0 + d = 0\n     \\]\n   - Set these equations equal to each other:\n     \\[\n     x_0^2 + bx_0 + c = x_0^2 + ax_0 + d\n     \\]\n   - Cancel out \\( x_0^2 \\) from both sides:\n     \\[\n     bx_0 + c = ax_0 + d\n     \\]\n   - Rearrange to isolate \\( x_0 \\):\n     \\[\n     bx_0 - ax_0 = d - c\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     (b - a)x_0 = d - c\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     x_0 = \\frac{d - c}{b - a}\n     \\]\n   - Since \\( 0 < a < b < c < d \\), it implies \n     \\[\n     d - c > 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b - a > 0\n     \\]\n     thus \n     \\[\n     x_0 = \\frac{d - c}{b - a} > 0\n     \\]\n   - However, using Vieta's formulas, for a quadratic \\( x^2 + px + q \\) with positive \\( p \\) and \\( q \\), the roots must have certain properties:\n     - Sum = \\( -p \\), thus if \\( p \\) is positive, roots must sum up to a negative number.\n     - Product = \\(