So neither ABC nor DEF is divisible by 37, but their sum is. Therefore, in such a case, we can't use cyclic permutations of ABC or DEF individually.\n\nBut in the case where ABC is divisible by 37, then DEF must also be, and we can cycle the digits of ABC and DEF to form new numbers. For example, if ABC is divisible by 37, then BCA and CAB are also divisible by 37. Similarly for DEF. Then, concatenating BCA and DEF, or CAB and DEF, or ABC and FED, etc., might give another six-digit number divisible by 37. But this depends on the specific case.\n\nBut the problem states that all digits are different. So even if ABC is divisible by 37, cycling its digits might lead to repetition in the full six-digit number? Wait, no, because if all six digits are different, then cycling ABC (which are three unique digits) would still keep them unique, and DEF (three other unique digits) would also remain unique. So, for example, if ABCDEF has all unique digits, then BCAFED would also have all unique digits. So that's a valid permutation.\n\nBut this only applies if ABC and DEF are each three-digit numbers with unique digits, which they are because all six digits are unique.\n\nHowever, this approach would only work if ABC is divisible by 37. But in the original problem, we don't know if ABC or DEF is divisible by 37. It's possible that neither is, but their sum is. Therefore, this method might not always apply.\n\nHmm, so perhaps we need a different approach.\n\nLet me recall that 37 divides 111, 222, ..., 999. These are all three-digit numbers with all digits the same. But in our problem, all digits are different, so those numbers are excluded.\n\nAnother thought: Since 37 is a prime, the multiplicative order of 10 modulo 37 is 3, since 10^3 \u2261 1 mod 37. Therefore, the powers of 10 modulo 37 cycle every 3. This periodicity might be useful.\n\nGiven that, maybe the value of the number modulo 37 depends on the digits grouped in threes. As we saw earlier, N \u2261 ABC + DEF mod 37.